---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout title="Student walkthroughs" description="A bespoke, self-paced guide to building and publishing your portfolio with GitHub, VS Code, and Copilot.">
<div class="container student-guide">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="eyebrow">MIS CLUB · WORKSHOP 01 · FIELD GUIDE</div>
    <h1>Build a portfolio.<br/><span class="hero-accent">Ship a real change.</span></h1>
    <p>Build a portfolio by completing one real software delivery journey: open the project, change it, review the diff, push the work, and verify the public site. Every mission has a checkpoint and a way back when something fails.</p>
    <div class="guide-actions"><a class="btn primary" href="#step-1">Start with the setup <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="btn" href="https://github.com/skyeshanohan/workshopsite/archive/refs/heads/portfolio-starter.zip">Get the clean starter ↓</a><a class="btn" href={base+'slides/'}>Open the deck ↗</a></div>
  </section>
  <section class="mission-map" aria-label="Workshop mission route">
    <div class="mission-map-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">THE ROUTE</div><h2>Six steps from local to live.</h2></div><p>Move forward when your checkpoint works. Skip ahead if you already have a working setup.</p></div>
    <div class="route-track" aria-label="Mission map">
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-1" data-route-step="1"><span class="route-node">01</span><b>Access</b><small>Get ready</small></a>
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-2" data-route-step="2"><span class="route-node">02</span><b>Claim</b><small>Own a repo</small></a>
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-3" data-route-step="3"><span class="route-node">03</span><b>Explore</b><small>Run locally</small></a>
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-4" data-route-step="4"><span class="route-node">04</span><b>Shape</b><small>Make it yours</small></a>
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-5" data-route-step="5"><span class="route-node">05</span><b>Review</b><small>Branch + PR</small></a>
      <a class="route-stop" href="#step-6" data-route-step="6"><span class="route-node">06</span><b>Launch</b><small>Verify live</small></a>
    </div>
    <div class="route-rescue">
      <div><b>Where are you right now?</b><span>Jump to the next useful guide instead of starting over.</span></div>
      <div class="rescue-options" role="group" aria-label="Choose your current situation">
        <button type="button" data-route-help="start">Starting fresh</button>
        <button type="button" data-route-help="local">Local site works</button>
        <button type="button" data-route-help="push">Push failed</button>
        <button type="button" data-route-help="actions">Deploy failed</button>
      </div>
      <p id="route-advice" aria-live="polite">Choose a situation for a suggested next move.</p>
      <a id="route-next-link" href="#step-1">Start at mission 01 →</a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="guide-status card">
    <div><div class="eyebrow">Progress tracker</div><h2 id="guide-progress-label">0 of 6 steps complete</h2><p class="muted small">Your checkpoints stay on this device. Nothing is sent to a server.</p></div>
    <div class="guide-progress"><div class="progress-wrap"><div class="progress-bar" id="guide-progress-bar"></div></div><span id="guide-progress-percent">0%</span></div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-1" data-step="1">
    <div class="step-number">01</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 01 · ACCESS + TOOLS</div><h2>Check your tools and accounts.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">Check the tools once. Then the rest of the workshop can focus on building instead of setup.</p>
      <div class="tool-cards tool-cards-five">
        <article><b>GitHub</b><p>Repository hosting and collaboration in your browser.</p><a href="https://github.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open GitHub ↗</a></article>
        <article><b>Git</b><p>Local version history: commits, branches, and diffs.</p><a href="https://git-scm.com/downloads" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Install Git ↗</a></article>
        <article><b>VS Code</b><p>The editor where you change files and review diffs.</p><a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/download" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Install VS Code ↗</a></article>
        <article><b>Node.js + npm</b><p>Runs the Astro starter and installs its packages. Use Node.js 22 for this workshop.</p><a href="https://nodejs.org/en/download" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Install Node.js ↗</a></article>
        <article><b>Copilot</b><p>Optional assistant for explanations and focused code changes.</p><a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/setup" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Copilot setup ↗</a></article>
      </div>
      <div class="tool-check">
        <div><span class="task-label">QUICK CHECK · OPEN VS CODE TERMINAL</span><pre id="tool-check-commands">git --version
node --version
npm --version</pre><button class="copy-button" id="copy-tool-check" type="button">Copy commands</button></div>
        <p><b>What you should see</b><br/>Three version numbers. For this starter, <code>node --version</code> should report a <code>v22.x.x</code> version. If a command says “not found,” use the install links above and restart the terminal.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Useful words</b><p><strong>Repository (repo):</strong> project files plus history. <strong>Commit:</strong> a saved checkpoint. <strong>Publish:</strong> make the site available at a web address.</p></div>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS</span><ol><li>Open your GitHub account in a browser.</li><li>Open VS Code on your computer.</li><li>In VS Code, check that the GitHub and Copilot accounts are the ones you plan to use.</li></ol><p class="help-text">If Copilot is not ready, keep going. You can follow the same steps without AI while the instructor helps.</p></div>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="1"/> I can open GitHub and VS Code.</label>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-2" data-step="2">
    <div class="step-number">02</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 02 · CLAIM YOUR REPOSITORY</div><h2>Create your repository.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">A repository is the home for your site. You will own this copy, so your changes will not affect another student.</p>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB · NEW REPOSITORY</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/creating-and-managing-repositories/quickstart-for-repositories" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/repository/create-repository-name.png" alt="Screenshot from GitHub Docs showing the real new-repository form and Repository name field." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Real GitHub UI from the official docs. Open <a href="https://github.com/new" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub’s New repository page ↗</a>; the fields may differ slightly from this documentation screenshot.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS</span><ol><li>Download the <strong>portfolio starter ZIP</strong> using the yellow button near the top of this page.</li><li>Extract the ZIP file. Open the new folder that contains <code>package.json</code> and the <code>src</code> folder in VS Code.</li><li>On GitHub, choose <strong>New repository</strong> (or <a href="https://github.com/new" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">open it directly ↗</a>). Give it a simple name such as <code>my-resume-site</code>.</li><li>Choose <strong>Public</strong> only if you are comfortable with the source code and website being visible to anyone. A public site should contain only details you are happy to share.</li><li>Create the repository without adding a README, license, or .gitignore. The starter already has project files.</li><li>In VS Code's terminal, run the commands below from the starter folder. Replace the example username and repository name with yours.</li></ol><p class="help-text">Copy one command at a time. The terminal runs commands on your computer; GitHub's website is where you create the empty remote repository.</p></div>
<div class="command-grid">
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>1 · Start Git history</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-init">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-init">git init</pre></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>2 · Stage the project files</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-add">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-add">git add .</pre></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>3 · Inspect what is staged</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-first-diff">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-first-diff">git status
git diff --staged</pre><p class="help-text">Check for passwords, tokens, private files, or anything you do not want to publish.</p></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>4 · Make the first commit</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-commit">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-commit">git commit -m "Create my portfolio"</pre></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>5 · Name the main branch</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-branch">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-branch">git branch -M main</pre></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>6 · Connect your GitHub repository</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-remote">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-remote">git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/YOUR-REPOSITORY.git</pre><p class="help-text">Change both capitalized placeholders first. Use the HTTPS URL shown on your repository page.</p></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>7 · Send your files to GitHub</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-push">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-push">git push -u origin main</pre></div>
</div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Why this matters</b><p>Your own repository means you can change the site, keep a history, and publish it under your own account or organization.</p></div>
      <details class="terminal-details">
        <summary>Git says “Author identity unknown”?</summary>
        <p>Git stores a name and email with each commit. These identify the commit author; they are not your GitHub password. For a public portfolio, consider using the GitHub noreply address shown in <b>GitHub → Settings → Emails</b>.</p>
        <div class="command-grid command-grid-short">
          <div class="command-card"><div><b>Set your commit name</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-user-name">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-user-name">git config --global user.name "Your Name"</pre></div>
          <div class="command-card"><div><b>Set your commit email</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-user-email">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-user-email">git config --global user.email "YOUR-GITHUB-NOREPLY-EMAIL"</pre><p class="help-text">Replace the placeholder with your GitHub noreply email, then retry the commit.</p></div>
        </div>
      </details>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="2"/> I have a repository for my own portfolio.</label>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-3" data-step="3">
    <div class="step-number">03</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 03 · OPEN THE ENGINE</div><h2>Run the project locally.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">GitHub stores the shared copy. VS Code lets you work on the files on your computer.</p>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>VISUAL STUDIO CODE · SOURCE CONTROL</span><a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/sourcecontrol/overview" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://code.visualstudio.com/assets/docs/sourcecontrol/overview/overview.png" alt="Real VS Code interface with the Source Control view and a side-by-side file diff." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Real VS Code screenshot. Select a changed file to read the diff before staging or committing it.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS</span><ol><li>In VS Code, open the folder that contains <code>package.json</code> and the <code>src</code> folder.</li><li>Open the integrated terminal: <strong>Terminal → New Terminal</strong>.</li><li>Install the starter's packages with <code>npm install</code>. This downloads the tools the project uses.</li><li>Start a local preview with <code>npm run dev</code>.</li><li>Open the local address printed in the terminal, usually <code>http://localhost:4321</code>.</li></ol><p class="help-text">Leave the terminal running while you preview the site. The local address works on your computer; it is not the public website yet.</p></div>
<div class="command-grid command-grid-short">
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>Install project tools</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-npm-install">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-npm-install">npm install</pre></div>
 <div class="command-card"><div><b>Start the local preview</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-npm-dev">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-npm-dev">npm run dev</pre></div>
</div>
      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPILOT PROMPT</span><h3>Ask Copilot to explain the files</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="explain-project">Copy prompt</button></div><pre id="explain-project">Explain this project in plain English. Tell me which file creates the page, which file controls the styles, and where I should edit my name, education, experience, skills, and projects. Do not change any files yet. Use short sentences.</pre></div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Read this before editing</b><p>It is okay if the code looks unfamiliar. First learn which file does what. Ask Copilot to explain; do not ask it to rewrite the entire project.</p></div>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="3"/> I can open the project and preview it locally.</label>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-4" data-step="4">
    <div class="step-number">04</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 04 · MAKE A REAL CHANGE</div><h2>Personalize the portfolio.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">Pick a design you like. Replace example content with information you want future employers or interviewers to see.</p>
      <div class="student-designs">
        <a href={base+'previews/editorial/'} class="student-design"><div class="design-swatch editorial-swatch"><small>01 / EDITORIAL</small><b>Thoughtful.<br/>By design.</b><i></i><i></i></div><strong>Editorial</strong><span>Warm, story-led, expressive.</span></a>
        <a href={base+'previews/developer/'} class="student-design"><div class="design-swatch developer-swatch"><small>02 / DEVELOPER</small><b>Build.<br/><em>Create_</em></b><i></i><i></i></div><strong>Developer</strong><span>Dark, technical, project-first.</span></a>
        <a href={base+'previews/professional/'} class="student-design"><div class="design-swatch professional-swatch"><small>03 / PROFESSIONAL</small><b>Jordan Lee</b><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><strong>Professional</strong><span>Structured, recruiter-friendly.</span></a>
      </div>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS</span><ol><li>Choose one design. You can change it later.</li><li>Update your name, short introduction, education, and skills.</li><li>Add one project. Class assignments are okay if you explain your contribution honestly.</li><li>Preview the page at desktop width and narrow/mobile width.</li><li>Check that your links point to the right places.</li></ol></div>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>VISUAL STUDIO CODE · COPILOT CHAT</span><a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/chat/copilot-chat-context" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://code.visualstudio.com/assets/docs/chat/copilot-chat/copilot-chat-view-chat-variables.png" alt="Real VS Code Copilot Chat screenshot showing the context picker for files and workspace context." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Real Copilot Chat interface from the VS Code docs. Add relevant file context, ask for one focused task, and review the proposed edits.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPILOT PROMPT</span><h3>Make one small improvement</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="one-improvement">Copy prompt</button></div><pre id="one-improvement">I am building a student resume portfolio. Please make one focused improvement: [describe the change]. Keep the existing Astro project structure and visual style. Do not add packages or rewrite unrelated files. First tell me which files you plan to change. After the change, explain what I should review and how to test it on mobile.</pre></div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Privacy check</b><p>Only publish details you are comfortable sharing publicly. Do not include your home address, passwords, API keys, private contact details, or information about another person without permission.</p></div>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="4"/> My site includes my own information and at least one project.</label>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-5" data-step="5">
    <div class="step-number">05</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 05 · REVIEW BEFORE YOU SHIP</div><h2>Commit, push, and review a change.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">A commit records a checkpoint. A pull request lets you review a change before it joins the main version of your project.</p>
      <div class="mini-workflow"><span>Issue</span><b>→</b><span>Branch</span><b>→</b><span>Commit</span><b>→</b><span>Pull request</span><b>→</b><span>Merge</span></div>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB · CREATE AN ISSUE</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/issues/tracking-your-work-with-issues/using-issues/creating-an-issue" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/issues/issue-title-body.png" alt="Real GitHub issue creation form with title and body fields filled in." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Real GitHub issue form. Write what should change and how you will prove it is done.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS</span><ol><li>On GitHub, open <strong>Issues</strong> and create a small task. Example: “Add a featured project card.”</li><li>Write a few checks that tell you when it is complete: a title, a short summary, a working link, and a layout that fits a phone screen.</li><li>Create a branch for this task. A branch is a separate line of work; it keeps this change organized.</li><li>Make the change in VS Code. Inspect the diff, then write a clear commit message.</li><li>Push the branch to GitHub and open a pull request.</li></ol></div>
      <section class="git-state-lab" aria-labelledby="git-state-title">
        <div class="lab-heading"><div><div class="eyebrow">INTERACTIVE WORKBENCH</div><h3 id="git-state-title">Understand what Git is tracking.</h3><p>Choose a state. The terminal output and next command change with it.</p></div><span class="lab-status">LOCAL REPO</span></div>
        <div class="git-state-track" role="group" aria-label="Git change lifecycle">
          <button type="button" class="git-state-button is-current" data-git-state="0" aria-pressed="true"><span>01</span><b>Edited</b><small>Working tree</small></button>
          <span class="state-connector" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <button type="button" class="git-state-button" data-git-state="1" aria-pressed="false"><span>02</span><b>Staged</b><small>Ready for commit</small></button>
          <span class="state-connector" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <button type="button" class="git-state-button" data-git-state="2" aria-pressed="false"><span>03</span><b>Committed</b><small>Saved to history</small></button>
          <span class="state-connector" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <button type="button" class="git-state-button" data-git-state="3" aria-pressed="false"><span>04</span><b>Pushed</b><small>Shared on GitHub</small></button>
        </div>
        <div class="git-state-detail">
          <div class="fake-terminal-real-meaning"><div class="terminal-chrome"><span>● ● ●</span><b>TERMINAL · EXAMPLE OUTPUT</b></div><pre id="git-state-output">On branch add-featured-project
Changes not staged for commit:
  modified: src/data/profile.js

no changes added to commit</pre></div>
          <div class="git-state-explanation"><span class="state-number" id="git-state-number">STATE 01 / 04</span><h4 id="git-state-heading">The edit exists only on your computer.</h4><p id="git-state-copy">Git can see the change, but it is not selected for the next commit.</p><div class="next-command"><div><span>NEXT COMMAND</span><code id="git-state-command">git add src/data/profile.js</code></div><button class="copy-button" type="button" id="copy-git-state">Copy command</button></div><p class="lab-small-note" id="git-state-note">Staging a file does not upload it to GitHub.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>
      <details class="terminal-details"><summary>Optional: copy the Git commands for the branch</summary><p>Use these if you want to work in the terminal. You can also use the Source Control panel in VS Code. Copy one command at a time.</p><div class="command-grid command-grid-short">
        <div class="command-card"><div><b>Create your task branch</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-feature-branch">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-feature-branch">git switch -c add-featured-project</pre></div>
        <div class="command-card"><div><b>Check which files changed</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-status">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-status">git status</pre></div>
        <div class="command-card"><div><b>Stage the changes you reviewed</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-feature-add">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-feature-add">git add src/data/profile.js</pre></div>
        <div class="command-card"><div><b>Save a checkpoint</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-feature-commit">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-feature-commit">git commit -m "Add featured project card"</pre></div>
        <div class="command-card"><div><b>Push the branch to GitHub</b><button class="copy-button" data-copy-command="cmd-feature-push">Copy</button></div><pre id="cmd-feature-push">git push -u origin add-featured-project</pre></div>
      </div></details>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB · REVIEW A PULL REQUEST</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/how-tos/review-pull-requests/reviewing-proposed-changes-in-a-pull-request" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/pull_requests/diff-settings-menu.png" alt="Real GitHub pull request Files changed view and diff settings." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Real GitHub review interface. Open <b>Files changed</b>, read what changed, and compare the diff with your issue before you merge.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPILOT PROMPT</span><h3>Review your own change</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="review-change">Copy prompt</button></div><pre id="review-change">Review my current changes against these acceptance checks: [paste your checks]. Look for broken links, mobile layout problems, accessibility issues, accidental secrets, and unrelated edits. Explain any problems in plain English. Do not make more changes until I review your findings.</pre></div>
      <div class="plain-note"><b>Before you merge</b><p>Read the changed files. Make sure the change matches your issue. Do not merge only because a button is green or Copilot says it is finished.</p></div>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="5"/> I recorded a change and reviewed a pull request (or followed the class checkpoint).</label>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="guide-step" id="step-6" data-step="6">
    <div class="step-number">06</div><div class="step-content">
      <div class="step-kicker">STEP 06 · GO LIVE</div><h2>Deploy and verify the live site.</h2>
      <p class="step-lede">Your source code is the recipe. The build creates the ready-to-serve files. GitHub Pages serves those files as a website.</p>
      <div class="deploy-illustration"><article><span>01</span><b>Your repository</b><small>Astro source files</small></article><div>→</div><article><span>02</span><b>GitHub Actions</b><small>Install + build</small></article><div>→</div><article><span>03</span><b>GitHub Pages</b><small>Live website</small></article></div>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB · PAGES PUBLISHED URL</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/configuring-a-publishing-source-for-your-github-pages-site" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/pages/click-pages-url-to-preview.png" alt="Real GitHub Pages settings and Visit site link for a published website." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Real GitHub Pages UI. Follow the published URL only after the latest build and deployment have succeeded.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure class="walkthrough-figure actual-product-screen"><div class="screen-label"><span>GITHUB ACTIONS · JOB OUTPUT</span><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/monitor-workflows/use-workflow-run-logs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official documentation ↗</a></div><img src="https://docs.github.com/assets/images/help/repository/actions-quickstart-log-detail.png" alt="Real GitHub Actions log view with an expanded step and output." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Real Actions log view. Open the failed job, expand the failed step, and read the first actionable error before editing code.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="step-task"><span class="task-label">DO THIS</span><ol><li>On your repository, open <strong>Settings</strong>, then <strong>Pages</strong>.</li><li>Under the publishing source, choose <strong>GitHub Actions</strong> when instructed by your starter setup.</li><li>Open the <strong>Actions</strong> tab and find the latest “Build and deploy” run.</li><li>Wait for the build and deployment steps to finish successfully.</li><li>Open the published URL. Check the homepage, one project link, and the mobile layout.</li></ol><p class="help-text">The first deploy can take a few minutes. If it fails, open the run and read the first error before changing anything.</p></div>
      <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-heading"><div><span class="prompt-label">COPILOT PROMPT</span><h3>Understand a deployment error</h3></div><button class="copy-button" data-copy-prompt="debug-deployment">Copy prompt</button></div><pre id="debug-deployment">Help me understand this build/deployment error in plain English: [paste the error text]. Tell me which step failed, the likely cause, and the smallest safe fix. Do not guess at settings that are not shown in the log.</pre></div>
      <label class="step-done"><input type="checkbox" data-step-check="6"/> My published website opens, or I know exactly which deployment step needs help.</label>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="finish-card">
    <div class="eyebrow">You made it</div><h2>You shipped it. Keep building.</h2><p>You have moved one change from an idea to a reviewed, public result. Workshop 2 adds automated checks to this same route. Keep the guide open when the next change arrives; the goal is to reuse the process, not memorize every command.</p>
